sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch12-07> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Each new protoplasmic drop settles in a position of minimum potential energy (including minimal area), which makes each chamber similar to the last; proportional growth of size then gives the series the form of a logarithmic spiral. Rhumbler's specific 'law of the constant angle' of contact is too narrow an explanation.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Minimum potential energy plus proportional growth yields the logarithmic spiral";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XII, “The Spiral Shells of the Foraminifera”; topic “Minimum potential and chamber similarity”. Scope: chambered Foraminifera"
}
